7.2  /tmp vs /var/tmp

TL;DR
  - /tmp: short-lived scratch files. Often in RAM (tmpfs) and emptied at every
    boot.
  - /var/tmp: temporary files that must survive a reboot. Always on disk.
  - Both are cleaned by age. With systemd's default settings, files unused for
    10 days leave /tmp, and 30 days for /var/tmp.
  - In scripts, create temporary files with mktemp, never with a fixed name.

      mktemp        # a new private file in /tmp
      mktemp -d     # a new private folder

On your machine
  /tmp      in RAM (tmpfs), emptied at every boot · 7.0 GiB free (88%) · inodes
            0% used
  /var/tmp  on disk (btrfs), part of / · 120.0 GiB free (30%)

Pitfalls
  - A tmpfs /tmp lives in RAM: a huge file there competes with your apps for
    memory. tmpfs is capped, at half the RAM unless set otherwise.
  - A fixed name like /tmp/myapp.log is a security hole: another user can create
    that name first, as a link to one of your files.
  - Everyone can write to /tmp, yet nobody can delete other people's files
    there: that's the sticky bit (see 9.2).
  - Don't keep anything you need in /tmp: it may be gone after a reboot.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why are there two temporary folders?

    The Filesystem Hierarchy Standard gives them different promises: /tmp may be
    cleared at boot, /var/tmp is kept between reboots. Programs that need large
    or long-lived temporary data, such as a package build or a text editor's
    recovery files, use /var/tmp.

  ▾ Why did distros move /tmp to RAM?

    RAM is faster than any disk, writes to RAM don't wear out an SSD, and
    emptying it at boot is free. Fedora made a tmpfs /tmp the default in release
    18 (2013), and Debian in release 13 (2025).

    The cost is memory: big files in /tmp take RAM. tmpfs can move them to swap
    under pressure, but a machine without swap has nowhere to put them.

Related
  Commands  mktemp
  Paths     /tmp, /var/tmp
  Hub       idk disk Disk
  See also  idk 9.2 setuid, setgid and sticky bit
  See also  idk 7.1 The hierarchy in 2 minutes
